/**
 * data.js
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The full A0 -> A1 -> A2 Spanish curriculum. This is the ONLY file
 * content editors touch to add sentences, lessons, units, or levels.
 * No UI code, no engine code lives here — just facts about content.
 *
 * IMPORTANT — how this plugs into the existing engine:
 * `lesson` is a single flat, monotonically increasing integer across the
 * WHOLE curriculum (level and unit boundaries don't reset it). That's the
 * one field progressEngine.js, testEngine.js and lessonEngine.js actually
 * read to build lessons, generate tests, and unlock content — exactly as
 * before this curriculum rebuild. `level`, `unit`, `category`, `type`,
 * `difficulty` and `tags` are pure metadata layered on top for the
 * dashboard's level/unit progress and for organizing future content —
 * removing them entirely wouldn't break lesson/test generation at all.
 *
 * To add a sentence: append an object with a new unique id, and give it
 * the `lesson` number it belongs to (existing or new).
 * To add a lesson: use a new `lesson` number — the app notices automatically.
 * To add a unit: append an entry to UNITS below.
 * To add content at a new level (e.g. starting A2): just tag new
 * sentences/lessons/units with level: 'A2' and add the level to LEVELS
 * if it isn't there yet.
 *
 * Note on ids vs lesson numbers: `id` only needs to be globally unique —
 * it does NOT need to be numerically close to its `lesson` number. That's
 * how new A0 content (ids 1201+) could be inserted at lessons 9-14
 * (between the original lessons 8 and 9) without renumbering a single id
 * of the other 1200 existing sentences — only their `lesson` field
 * shifted. Sentences are grouped into lessons purely by `.lesson`, so
 * insertion order in this array doesn't matter either.
 *
 * Shape:
 * {
 *   id,              unique integer across the whole language
 *   languageCode,    must match a code in LANGUAGES (config.js)
 *   level,           'A0' | 'A1' | 'A2' | ... — CEFR-ish level tag
 *   unit,            unit number (see UNITS below) — semantic grouping,
 *                    purely for display/dashboard, not read by the engine
 *   lesson,          FLAT lesson number across the entire curriculum
 *   type,            'word' | 'phrase' | 'sentence' | 'question' | 'expression'
 *   category,        topic slug, matches its unit
 *   difficulty,      1 (easiest) .. 5
 *   target,          sentence in the target language
 *   pronunciation,   phonetic guide (Arabic)
 *   english,         English translation shown as the prompt
 *   answers          array of accepted spoken/typed answers (gender
 *                    variants, alternate spellings, etc.)
 * }
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------
 */
